This mutation changes how a MOVE is scored. A step now earns more the further it closes the gap to the goals, and it is penalised when it ends next to an enemy champion. `progress` is unchanged. The enemy-adjacency check is the only added engine call, so it is the main fuel cost.

```python
@gene("s1", "value of doing this option now: moves scale with progress, penalised next to enemy champions", parents=["s1"])
def score(o: Option) -> Num:
    if o.kind == PICK:
        return 1
    if o.kind == BUY:
        return 100 if o.item == 6 else 0
    if o.kind == RECALL:
        return 90 if me().health_frac < 0.35 else 0
    if o.kind == ATTACK:
        if o.unit.is_champion:
            return 80
        return 60 if gems() == 0 else 0
    if o.kind == TRIBUTE:
        return 70
    if o.kind == MOVE:
        p = progress(o.tile)
        if p <= 0:
            return 0
        risk = count(1 for u in enemies() if u.is_champion and dist(u.pos, o.tile) <= 2)
        return 40 + 10 * p - 30 * risk
    return 0
```

This mutation makes `progress` a safety-weighted step. A step still earns the distance gained toward the goals, but each enemy within 3 tiles of the destination costs 3 points. The MOVE score's `progress(o.tile) > 0` gate stays as is, so a step that gains ground but walks into enemy reach is no longer valued as highly. The cost is one extra scan of `enemies()` per candidate step; I kept it direct rather than adding a per-turn keep to avoid stale-set bugs.

```python
@gene("s4", "distance gained toward goals minus a penalty for enemies near the destination", parents=["s4"])
def progress(t: Tile) -> Num:
    return at(goal_field(), me().pos) - at(goal_field(), t) - 3 * count(1 for u in enemies() if dist(u.pos, t) <= 3)
```

